# Service Backup

Containerized backup system that mirrors each node's `/opt/data/<service>`
config directories to the NAS as dated, space-efficient snapshots via an
rsync daemon. Deployed per-node through Portainer, configured entirely
through environment variables — no code changes needed to add a node.

## Where it lives

- **Source code:** private git repo `service-backup` (GitHub)
- **Built image:** `registry.local.my-domain.com/service-backup:latest`
  (see [Docker Registry](docker-registry) for registry details)
- **Deployed via:** Portainer stack, one per node
- **Backup destination:** Synology NAS `longsnapper.gs`, rsync daemon
  module `backup` → `/volume1/homes/hometeam/backups/`

## How it works

- Each service's config directory is mirrored via `rsync` over the
  Synology's rsync daemon (not SSH — DSM's SSH had too much friction;
  rsyncd is simpler and doesn't need shell access).
- **Snapshot retention**: every run creates a new timestamped directory
  (`<service>/<YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS>/`) rather than overwriting one mirror.
  Unchanged files are hard-linked against the previous snapshot
  (`--link-dest`), so multiple days of snapshots cost roughly one full
  copy plus deltas, not a full copy per day.
- **Retention window** is controlled by `BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS` — old
  snapshots beyond that window get pruned automatically.
- Runs on a schedule inside the container via `supercronic` (chosen over
  plain cron/dcron, which crashed under the container runtime here due
  to a blocked `setpgid` syscall).

## Deploying to a new node

1. On the node, clone the repo (or just grab `docker-compose.yml` +
   `.env.example`):
```bash
   git clone git@github.com:griffinsteffy19/service-backup.git
   cd service-backup
   cp .env.example .env
```
2. Edit `.env` — at minimum set:
   - `NODE_NAME` — unique per node
   - `HOST_SOURCES_PATH` — host path containing service folders (e.g. `/opt/data`)
   - `SERVICES` — comma-separated list of service folder names to back up
   - `SERVICE_EXCLUDES_<NAME>` — optional per-service exclude patterns
   - `REMOTE_TARGETS` / `RSYNC_PASSWORD` — same NAS target as other nodes
3. Deploy as a Portainer stack:
   - Paste `docker-compose.yml` into a new stack
   - Paste `.env` contents into the stack's Environment variables section
   - Deploy
4. Verify:
```bash
   docker exec service-backup-<node-name> /usr/local/bin/backup.sh
   docker logs -f service-backup-<node-name>
```
   Confirm `[SUCCESS]` for each expected service, then check the NAS side:
```bash
   rsync --list-only rsync://hometeam@longsnapper.gs/backup/<node-name>/
```

## Key `.env` variables

| Variable | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `NODE_NAME` | Identifies this node's folder on the backup target |
| `HOST_SOURCES_PATH` | Host-side path bind-mounted into the container as `/sources` |
| `SERVICES` | Comma-separated service folder names to back up |
| `SERVICE_EXCLUDES_<NAME>` | Space-separated exclude patterns per service (name uppercased, hyphens → underscores) |
| `BACKUP_MODE` | `snapshot` (dated, retained) |
| `BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS` | How many days of snapshots to keep |
| `REMOTE_TARGETS` | `rsync://user@host/module` — the rsync daemon target |
| `RSYNC_PASSWORD` | Password matching the NAS's rsyncd secrets file (**not** the account's real DSM login password) |
| `CRON_SCHEDULE` | Standard cron syntax, when the backup runs |
| `RUN_ON_START` | If `true`, runs a backup immediately on container start (useful for first deploy/testing, `false` for steady-state) |

## NAS-side setup (one-time, already done for `longsnapper.gs`)

- rsync service enabled: Control Panel → File Services → rsync
- Module defined in `/etc/rsyncd/rsyncd.conf`:
```ini
  [backup]
      path = /volume1/homes/hometeam/backups
      read only = no
      list = yes
      auth users = hometeam
      secrets file = /etc/rsyncd/rsyncd.secrets
      strict modes = false
      uid = hometeam
      gid = users
```
- Credentials in `/etc/rsyncd/rsyncd.secrets` (`hometeam:<password>`, `chmod 600`)
- Restart via Control Panel toggle (this DSM version doesn't have `synoservicectl`/`synosystemctl`)

## Known gotchas (worth remembering)

- **`--mkpath` isn't supported** by this NAS's bundled rsync (3.1.2) — the
  script instead pre-creates nested remote directories using `-R`
  (relative path trick) before the main transfer.
- **`--no-g --no-o` is required** on the rsync transfer — without it,
  rsync tries to preserve original file group/owner (from each service's
  container UID/GID) on the NAS side, which fails with `Operation not
  permitted` since the rsyncd module runs as a fixed `uid`/`gid`.
- **Docker build caching can silently ship a stale image** — if a fix
  doesn't seem to take effect after rebuild/push/redeploy, verify locally
  first:
```bash
  grep -c <thing-you-fixed> backup.sh   # confirm the source file changed
  docker build --no-cache -t <image> .  # bypass cache entirely
  docker images <image> --format '{{.ID}}'  # confirm digest actually changed
```
- **Heredoc pastes over SSH can silently truncate/corrupt scripts** —
  if a syntax error appears after editing via heredoc, verify with
  `bash -n backup.sh` before rebuilding; prefer `scp`-ing a locally
  edited file over pasting large heredocs through an SSH session.

## Manual on-demand run (debugging)

```bash
docker exec service-backup-<node-name> /usr/local/bin/backup.sh
```
Runs immediately without waiting for cron or restarting the container.
